  "text": "Camrelizumab-based regimens, such as camrelizumab monotherapy and camrelizumab plus chemotherapy or targeted therapy, achieve acceptable treatment response in esophageal cancer patients ([17]; [19]; [18]; [20]; [21]). For example, camrelizumab combined with apatinib and chemotherapy as a first-line treatment achieved ORR and DCR of 80.0% and 96.7%, respectively, in advanced ESCC patients [21]. Additionally, the ESCORT-1st study reported that camrelizumab plus chemotherapy as a first-line treatment achieved an ORR of 72.1% and a DCR of 91.3% in advanced or metastatic ESCC patients [18]. Moreover, the CAP 02 Re-challenge study indicated that the ORR and DCR were 10.2% and 69.4% in advanced ESCC patients who had a history of immune checkpoint inhibitors [20]. Overall, the previously reported range of ORR was 10.2%\u201380.0%, and the range of DCR was 69.4%\u201396.7% in advanced esophageal cancer patients receiving camrelizumab-based regimens ([17]; [19]; [18]; [20]; [21]). In this study, the ORR and DCR were 18.8% and 84.4%, respectively, in advanced esophageal cancer patients. The ORR and DCR identified in this study were within the ranges reported by previous studies ([17]; [19]; [18]; [20]; [21]). Therefore, in real-world clinical practice, camrelizumab-based regimens could exhibit potentially satisfactory efficacy in advanced esophageal cancer patients. According to previous real-world clinical studies, the ORR and DCR ranged from 15.1% to 72.0%

